{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering effort to reduce penalties for controlled substances will end this weekend when a revised legal framework reinstates penalties for minor drug offenses. Under the updated legislation, individuals possessing small quantities—such as fentanyl, heroin, or meth—will face either prosecution or a mandatory treatment path, which demands enrollment in a behavioral intervention program alongside a diversion initiative to bypass potential penalties. Though personal possession remains classified as a misdemeanor with a maximum six-month imprisonment term, lawmakers emphasize stricter enforcement in public spaces, particularly near recreational areas like parks, while imposing tougher consequences for drug sales in these zones.\n\nCounties are urged, though not legally bound, to establish treatment options to funnel offenders away from the judicial system toward rehabilitation and mental health support. Supporters argue this flexibility lets local governments tailor solutions to their available funding, whereas critics warn it may result in inconsistent enforcement across regions. Portland’s police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the strict criteria for diversion, noting that eligibility excludes those with additional charges, outstanding warrants, or a history of violent actions, or who are medically unstable.\n\nOfficers will screen qualifying cases through a county deflection hotline, sharing basic details to assess suitability before dispatching a mobile outreach team for behavioral health assessment. However, Day noted a strict 30-minute window for these teams to respond, after which arrested individuals would be processed through the justice system. The absence of extended wait times underscores the urgency of the new approach, even as it maintains the possibility of incarceration.\n\n***\nP.P.S. Meanwhile, in unrelated developments, a small business near the border has adjusted its hours to align with neighboring jurisdictions, prompting conversations about how shared policies might affect residents of all *age* groups. While local officials discuss potential changes, many vendors remain unsure how these shifts will impact their ability to *eat* in crowded *shopping* plazas. Additionally, a language debate has surfaced over whether signs in public spaces should feature multilingual labels, with some arguing it respects diverse *tongue* preferences while others call it unnecessary. The discussion mirrors broader *country*-wide tensions over cultural integration and accessibility.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.049770671874284744}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.378782902378589e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08514422923326492}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8014369010925293}, {"token": " effort", "logprob": -1.7965424060821533}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.01459505409002304}, {"token": " reduce", "logprob": -1.4244223833084106}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.1754915714263916}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.011284928768873215}, {"token": " controlled", "logprob": -2.2507684230804443}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.008646194823086262}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.9314087629318237}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.3795347809791565}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.3292630910873413}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.16976647078990936}, {"token": " when", "logprob": -2.8393595218658447}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.6089739203453064}, {"token": " revised", "logprob": -0.8823757171630859}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -0.6798445582389832}, {"token": " framework", "logprob": -0.3383446931838989}, {"token": " rein", "logprob": -0.5576168298721313}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.006152025423943996}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -3.1816606521606445},